WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.125 --> 00:00:20.128
The woke me at daybreak, and I then woke my wife.

2
00:00:22.209 --> 00:00:25.750
In the quiet interval before the boys were up, we discussed our situation.

3
00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:28.791
"Do you think any sailors reached land?"

4
00:00:29.191 --> 00:00:29.851
she asked me.

5
00:00:29.871 --> 00:00:33.712
"I don't know, but we need to find out," I replied.

6
00:00:34.444 --> 00:00:37.286
"We also need to learn more about this place where we're stranded."

7
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:41.889
We decided that Fritz and I would start on an expedition after breakfast.

8
00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:45.831
My wife would remain near our landing place with the other boys.

9
00:00:49.674 --> 00:00:52.716
After packing up some food and water, Fritz and I set out.

10
00:00:57.178 --> 00:00:59.580
We walked upstream until we reached a waterfall.

11
00:01:00.476 --> 00:01:04.239
Then we crossed the stream using several large rocks as stepping stones.

12
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.183
On the other side of the stream was a field of tall grass.

13
00:01:08.983 --> 00:01:12.326
We had just begun to force our way through the grass when we heard a rustling sound.

14
00:01:13.147 --> 00:01:17.530
Turning around, we saw the grass waving as if some animal were passing through.

15
00:01:18.531 --> 00:01:20.113
Fritz brought his gun to his shoulder.

16
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:26.098
"Turk!" cried Fritz, lowering his gun as the dog approached.

17
00:01:26.118 --> 00:01:28.680
"It's a good thing you didn't fire," I said.

18
00:01:29.632 --> 00:01:33.094
The three of us headed toward the beach to search for any sign of the sailors.

19
00:01:33.915 --> 00:01:38.518
We pushed on until we came to a grove of trees stretching down to the water's edge.

20
00:01:39.359 --> 00:01:42.341
A thousand brightly colored birds twittered in the branches above.

21
00:01:43.241 --> 00:01:47.284
As we gazed up at them, Fritz stumbled over a round object on the ground.

22
00:01:51.427 --> 00:01:53.288
must be a bird's nest," he said.

23
00:01:53.308 --> 00:01:56.810
"Every round, hairy thing is not a bird's nest."

24
00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:57.591
I laughed.

25
00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:01.245
"This is a coconut, and it should be full of delicious liquid."

26
00:02:02.006 --> 00:02:04.928
We split the coconut open, but found that it had dried up.

27
00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.933
Moving on, we came to some trees with strange bumps on their trunks.

28
00:02:11.953 --> 00:02:17.677
"Those are calabash trees," I said, recognizing them from travel books.

29
00:02:17.697 --> 00:02:19.658
"The bumps are gourds.

30
00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:24.802
The natives make bowls, spoons, and bottles from gourds, and even cooking vessels."

31
00:02:24.822 --> 00:02:26.663
"That's impossible!"

32
00:02:26.923 --> 00:02:27.664
Fritz pointed out.

33
00:02:28.344 --> 00:02:30.525
"A gourd would burn if it were set on the fire."

34
00:02:30.545 --> 00:02:34.346
"The natives don't set the gourd on the fire," I replied.

35
00:02:34.366 --> 00:02:36.666
"They cut it in two and hollow it out.

36
00:02:37.107 --> 00:02:38.867
Then they fill it with water and food.

37
00:02:39.567 --> 00:02:43.208
Next they add red-hot stones until the water boils and the food is done."

38
00:02:43.228 --> 00:02:44.889
"How clever!"

39
00:02:45.149 --> 00:02:45.689
said Fritz.

40
00:02:46.529 --> 00:02:49.490
We cut some gourds and left them to pick up on our way home.

41
00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.111
For three hours we kept walking.

42
00:02:53.011 --> 00:02:55.892
At last we came to a piece of land that stretched into the sea.

43
00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.024
From its rocky summit, I knew we could obtain a good view of the surrounding country.

44
00:03:02.745 --> 00:03:04.467
We reached the top with little difficulty.

45
00:03:05.287 --> 00:03:09.911
Before us stretched a wide and lovely bay, and the scene inland was no less beautiful.

46
00:03:11.653 --> 00:03:15.576
But both Fritz and I felt lonely as we realized we were the only people there.

47
00:03:15.596 --> 00:03:17.838
"Cheer up, Fritz," I said.

48
00:03:18.699 --> 00:03:23.903
"Remember that we chose a settler's life when we left Switzerland, although we didn't expect to be so

49
00:03:24.063 --> 00:03:24.784
utterly alone."

50
00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.081
We descended the hill and headed toward a clump of palm trees.

51
00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:32.983
But to reach them we had to pass through a dense thicket of reeds.

52
00:03:33.903 --> 00:03:37.665
Fearing that we might encounter a snake, I broke off a reed for a weapon.

53
00:03:38.705 --> 00:03:40.886
I soon noticed liquid dripping from one end.

54
00:03:41.646 --> 00:03:44.988
When I tasted it, I realized the reeds were sugarcanes.

55
00:03:45.948 --> 00:03:48.469
"Fritz, we must cut some of these for Mother."

56
00:03:52.371 --> 00:03:55.512
We cut as many as we could carry and continued toward the palm trees.

57
00:03:57.624 --> 00:04:02.806
As we emerged from the sugarcane thicket, we spotted a troop of monkeys playing under the palm trees.

58
00:04:03.986 --> 00:04:05.887
They quickly climbed to the top of the trees.

59
00:04:05.907 --> 00:04:08.387
"Monkeys are natural mimics," I said.

60
00:04:08.407 --> 00:04:09.207
"Watch this."

61
00:04:10.188 --> 00:04:13.029
I gathered some stones and tossed them up toward the monkeys.

62
00:04:14.169 --> 00:04:18.090
They instantly seized all the coconuts within reach and tossed them down to us.

63
00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.431
Fritz was delighted with my trick and gathered up some of the finest coconuts.

64
00:04:23.312 --> 00:04:28.840
These were fresh, so I made some holes in the shells and we enjoyed the delicious liquid inside.

65
00:04:29.881 --> 00:04:33.804
Then I cut the coconuts in two and we ate the meat that lined their shells.

66
00:04:34.705 --> 00:04:36.026
It was now time to head home.

67
00:04:37.047 --> 00:04:40.130
We walked slowly with our load of sugarcanes and coconuts.

68
00:04:42.692 --> 00:04:46.596
Turk bounded ahead of us and surprised another troop of monkeys playing on the ground.

69
00:04:47.456 --> 00:04:51.120
They all escaped except for a little one, who tried to hide in the grass.

70
00:04:52.556 --> 00:04:57.499
Fritz rushed to its rescue, flinging away all he was carrying and losing his hat in his haste.

71
00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:03.683
With one leap, the monkey was on his shoulders, holding tight to Fritz's thick, curly hair.

72
00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:07.706
"Help!" cried Fritz as he tried to pull the creature off.

73
00:05:07.726 --> 00:05:09.487
"Help me, Father!"
